test, which verifies a valid dependency chain resolves to \"base,lib,app\" and exercises several error cases: a missing dependency, a circular pair, a duplicate name, and an empty name field—each of these should return an error from Build.\n\nI need to figure out where each error case gets detected: missing dependencies and cycles belong in graph.Order(), while duplicate names and empty names belong in parse.Load(). Now I'm thinking through the cache design, which needs to be keyed by input data so it can detect when invalidation should occur based on content changes.\n\nThe test shows that calling Build with different inputs returns different cached results, errors shouldn't be cached (so a broken input always returns an error on retry), and identical inputs later should still hit the cache correctly. So the cache needs to key by input, skip caching when there's an error, and return the stored result for matching inputs.\n\nFor the batch implementation, I'm planning to iterate over a single cache instance across all inputs and fail fast if any item errors, returning nil and the error together. The test seems to check that a batch containing one broken entry alongside a valid one causes the whole batch call to fail.\n\nI'm also noting that the cache must persist across repeated inputs within a batch, since the same input string appears twice and should hit cache the second time. For main.go, I need to switch to using Println for proper newline termination and make sure nonzero exit codes are returned on errors.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":515,"cache_read_input_tokens":18334,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":515},"output_tokens":12,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95ae646a-2832-4a85-8ae5-c0115c95a7